% English translation update of R823 lines 14316--14719.
\section*{30. Abstract Construction of Ideal Theory in Algebraic Number and Function Fields.}
\noindent\emph{Math. Ann. 96 (1927), pp.~26--61.}

\setcounter{footnote}{0}

\begin{center}
By\\[0.5em]
Emmy Noether in Göttingen.
\end{center}

In what follows an abstract characterization is given of all those rings whose ideal
theory agrees with the ideal theory of all integers of an algebraic number field:
that is, whose ideals can be represented uniquely as products of powers of prime
ideals. Among the best-known examples of such rings are also the ring of all
integers of an algebraic function field in one indeterminate, and, more generally,
in several indeterminates as soon as, following Kronecker, one restricts to ideals
of highest dimension, i.e. passes to a certain quotient ring, the functional domain.

For the underlying commutative ring, the axioms completely equivalent to this ideal
theory are the following:\footnote{For the definition of the basic concepts of ideal
theory, compare for instance E. Noether, \emph{Idealtheorie in Ringbereichen},
Math. Ann. 83 (1921), pp. 24--66 (cited below as \emph{Ideal Theory}). Incidentally,
the most essential concepts are also formulated briefly in the present paper,
especially in §§1, 4, and 5. For completeness, some material is included that is not
needed for the immediate purposes of this paper.}

\begin{description}
\item[I. Divisor-chain condition:] Every chain of ideals in which each ideal is a
proper divisor of the preceding one terminates after finitely many steps; in other
words, in every divisor chain of ideals there is an index from which on all ideals
are equal.
\item[II. Multiple-chain condition modulo every nonzero ideal.] Every chain of ideals
— all of which are divisors of a fixed ideal different from the zero ideal — in which
each ideal is a proper multiple of the preceding one terminates after finitely many
steps.
\item[III.] Existence of the identity element for multiplication.
\item[IV.] Ring without zero divisors.
\item[V. Integral closedness in the quotient field:] Every element of the quotient
field that is integral with respect to the ring belongs to the ring.
\end{description}

From these axioms I develop, step by step, an increasingly restricted ideal theory
up to the desired one; conversely, I show that all these axioms are in fact satisfied
there.

From the divisor-chain condition I it follows, as I showed in \emph{Ideal Theory}
§4, that an ideal is representable as the least common multiple of finitely many
primary ideals belonging to distinct prime ideals. I recall this proof briefly here
(§6), both because it can be simplified by using the concept of the ideal quotient,
and because I want to correct an oversight: the original proof uses, at one point,
the not-assumed existence of the identity element of multiplication.\footnote{P.
Urysohn called my attention to this oversight; my own alteration of the proof was
somewhat more cumbersome than the one given here, which is due to E. Artin.
He had already filled the gap for himself earlier, and, independently of me, had also
observed the simplification with the ideal quotient. — I take a further simplification,
which avoids the introduction of the “reduced representation,” from a related question
in W. Krull, \emph{Algebraische Theorie der Ringe III}, Math. Ann. 92 (1924),
pp. 181--213, Theorem I.}

The assumption of the multiple-chain condition has the effect (§7) that, in the residue
class ring modulo every ideal different from the zero ideal, a prime ideal cannot have
a proper divisor, except for the unit ideal containing all elements. Consequently the
primary components of these ideals are determined uniquely, except for those
belonging to the unit ideal. In a somewhat less sharp form this result is already
found in Masazo Sono;\footnote{Masazo Sono, \emph{On the Reduction of Ideals},
Memoirs of the College of Science, Kyoto University, Series A, 7 (1924),
pp. 191--204.} his assumption of the existence of a composition series in the residue
class ring is equivalent to the “double chain condition” in this ring, as I show briefly
at the end (§10). By double chain condition I mean the validity of both the divisor-chain
and multiple-chain conditions without restriction to ideals different from the zero ideal.

If axiom III — existence of the identity element — is also satisfied, the unit ideal
does not occur as an associated prime ideal. The primary components are therefore
uniquely determined; they are pairwise coprime, and their least common multiple
equals their product. Axioms I, II, III hold for all finite orders of an algebraic
number field; already Dedekind (Dirichlet--Dedekind, \emph{Zahlentheorie}, 3rd ed.,
11th supplement, §172), without giving the proof in full detail, stated here the
unique representation of an ideal as a product of pairwise coprime one-kind ideals
(primary components).

From assumption IV — absence of zero divisors — it follows that the different
powers of an ideal distinct from both the zero ideal and the unit ideal are all distinct,
and that the quotient field exists. If finally axiom V of integral closedness in the
quotient field is also satisfied, the primary ideals — uniquely determined because
of IV — become powers of prime ideals, and the usual factorization theorem is
obtained (§8). This latter proof rests on a consequence of integral closedness already
drawn by Dedekind (3rd ed., §172) in the case of number fields: a genuinely
fractional element can be represented as a quotient of integers in such a way that
even the square of the numerator is not divisible by the denominator. In later
presentations this consequence is replaced — likewise as a consequence of integral
closedness — by the much less transparent generalized Gaussian theorem or by
corresponding module theorems that say somewhat more; all of these are theorems
whose application presupposes basis elements, whereas here one works directly with
the ideals themselves.

In the converse direction \hbox{\(\S 9\)}, the axioms distinct from axiom V follow almost
directly; V follows from the proof that a genuinely fractional element can always
be represented so that no power of the numerator is divisible by the denominator,
which is therefore a strengthening of the consequence originally derived from V.
This proof succeeds only after one draws from the ideal representation the usual
conclusions: representation by principal ideals modulo a fixed ideal, and the theory
of fractional ideals, i.e. of ideal quotients in the field. The fact that integral closedness
follows from the representation by powers of prime ideals was also already known
to Dedekind, as is shown by a remark in the 3rd edition, §172, p. 522, bottom.

Axioms I through V imply that the four decompositions which are generally distinct
— into least pairwise coprime ideals, into mutually prime ideals, into largest primary
components, and into irreducible ideals — coincide; conversely, from this coincidence
and axioms I--IV, integral closedness follows. For rings with zero divisors, fulfillment
of the remaining axioms — with V defined as integral closedness in the quotient ring
— does not necessarily imply that the decompositions coincide, as the example of the
residue class ring modulo certain ideals of a finite order shows \hbox{\(\S 9\)}.

In §1 I sketch the theory of quantities integral with respect to a ring, up to
Dedekind's consequence from integral closedness. In §2 I show how the chain
conditions are transferred from the ring to modules consisting of linear forms, with
this ring as coefficient and multiplier domain.\footnote{This form of the module
theorems is due to Prüfer (Neue Begründung der algebraischen Zahlentheorie, §2,
Math. Ann. 94 (1924), pp. 198--243) and is more transparent than the customary
transfer of the basis theorems.} From this I draw (§3) the conclusion that, on
passing to the integral elements of an algebraic extension field of the first kind,
axioms I to IV remain valid for every order, while for the order consisting of all
integral elements, V is also valid. This fact — of course familiar for the algebraic
number field — permits the subordination of the function fields mentioned at the
beginning; in particular, by the simple proof that the functional domain derived from
integral polynomials in several indeterminates satisfies the axioms, Kronecker's
ideal theory is fully subsumed.\footnote{The difference appears only in the discriminant
theorems, because the residue class ring of the functional domain modulo a prime
number becomes an imperfect field, and hence extensions of the second kind can occur.}

The ideal theory subsequently developed does not presuppose these §§2 and 3, which
serve only for classification. In §4 and §5 the foundations independent of axioms I
through V are given; this brings out more sharply than in the original foundation
which parts of the theory are independent of finiteness assumptions.

\begin{center}
{\bfseries\large § 1.\par}
\vspace{0.35em}
{\bfseries\large Theory of Integral Quantities.\par}
\end{center}

Let there be given a commutative ring\footnote{As is known, a ring is defined once
an equality relation is given in a set, and in addition two operations, addition and
multiplication, are given satisfying the usual laws: addition is associative,
commutative, and uniquely reversible; multiplication is associative — commutative
when the ring is commutative — and distributive with respect to addition. If the
underlying definition of equality is not set-theoretic identity, then, so that the same
definition of equality is retained in every subsystem, such a subsystem (subring,
module, ideal) must contain with any element all elements equal to it. Likewise,
in every extension it must be assumed that the new definition of equality includes
the old one. All concepts such as “finitely many elements,” “unique assignment,”
etc. are meant in the sense of the equality definition; for example, “there is one and
only one element” means that there is only one element up to equality. Incidentally,
one can always pass from equality to set-theoretic identity by collecting equal
elements into a class and introducing these classes as the new elements. The remarks
given here on the definition of equality are due to R. Hölzer.} $\mT$ without zero
divisors, with identity element $e$ for multiplication (axioms III and IV); in $\mT$
a fixed subring $\mR$ containing the identity element is distinguished. The concepts
module, order, integral are throughout to refer to this fixed ring $\mR$, which later
will be omitted from the notation for brevity.\footnote{The theory of integral
quantities remains valid if zero divisors are admitted, provided that in their place
one assumes the divisor-chain condition in $\mR$, which by §2 also makes the proof
of the lemma possible. Since this form of the theory of integral quantities is not
used later, I only point to it in footnotes. All definitions under discussion remain
valid in the presence of zero divisors; most of them require — as is known and
easy to see — even weaker assumptions. What is not retained is Dedekind's formulation:
“A number is integral when it has a hull.” For with zero divisors present, the
quotient of two finite modules need not remain finite; hence here the order is
defined directly and not as a module quotient.} (Elements of $\mR$ will be denoted
by Latin letters, elements of $\mT$ in general by Greek letters.)

1. A system $\mM$ of elements of $\mT$ is called, as usual, an $\mR$-module — briefly,
a module — if, together with two elements $\alpha$ and $\beta$, it also contains their
difference, and with $\alpha$ also $r\alpha$, where $r$ is any element of $\mR$.
$\mM$ is said to be divisible by $\mN$,
\[
        \mM\equiv 0(\mN),
\]
and $\mM$ is called a multiple, $\mN$ a divisor, if every element of $\mM$ is also contained
in $\mN$. $\mR$-modules all of whose elements belong to $\mR$ are called ideals in $\mR$.

Clearly the intersection — the least common multiple $[\ldots,\mM_i,\ldots]$ — of
any number of modules from $\mT$ is again a module; likewise $\mT$ itself is a module.
Thus the module derived from any system $\Sigma$ of elements of $\mT$ exists uniquely
as the intersection of all modules containing $\Sigma$. The sum — the greatest
common divisor $(\ldots,\mM_i,\ldots)$ — of any system of modules is the module
derived from their union. The product $\mM\mN$ of two modules is defined as the
module derived from the elements $\mu\nu$, where $\mu$ runs through all elements
of $\mM$ and $\nu$ through all elements of $\mN$. The product therefore satisfies the
associative and commutative laws, since these hold for the ring elements. The
distributive law in $\mT$ further gives, for modules, the distributive law
\[
        (\ldots,\mM_i,\ldots)\mN=(\ldots,\mM_i\mN,\ldots),
\]
since by exactly this law in $\mT$ both sides are the module derived from all elements
$\mu_i\alpha$.

Since $\mR$ itself is a module, the condition that $\mR$ is the multiplier domain can
be expressed by $\mR\mM\equiv0(\mM)$. Since by hypothesis $\mR$ contains the identity
element, one also has $\mM\equiv0(\mR\mM)$, and hence $\mM=\mR\mM$.

A module $\mM$ is called finite if there is at least one system $\Sigma$ consisting
of finitely many elements from which $\mM$ is derived; the elements
$\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_r$ of $\Sigma$ are called a module basis of
\[
        \mM=(\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_r).
\]
The sum and product of two — and hence of finitely many — finite modules are
again finite, since they are derived from the union, respectively from the products,
of the basis elements. The latter assertion follows from the representation
$r_1\mu_1+\cdots+r_k\mu_k$ of all elements $\mu$ of $\mM$ by the basis\footnote{If
$\mR$ contains no identity element, the basis representation takes the form
$r_1\mu_1+\cdots+r_k\mu_k+n_1\mu_1+\cdots+n_k\mu_k$, where the integers $n$
are not ring elements but symbols for repeated addition or subtraction.} and from
the commutative law of multiplication in $\mT$, both of which are used here for
the first time.

2. An extension ring of $\mR$ lying in $\mT$ — hence one that contains all elements
of $\mR$ — is called an $\mR$-order, briefly an order. The intersection of arbitrarily
many orders in $\mT$ is again an order, and likewise $\mT$ is an order; hence the order
derived from any system $\Sigma$ of elements of $\mT$ exists uniquely. Sums and
products of orders are defined correspondingly as for modules; in particular
$\mO^2=\mO$ for every order.

Every order is at the same time an $\mR$-module; an order is called finite when it is
a finite module. Since sums and products arise by forming module sums and products,
sums and products of finite orders are again finite orders by 1.; further, the following
transitive law holds: if $\mA$ is a finite $\mR$-order and $\mB$ is a finite $\mA$-order,
then $\mB$ is also a finite $\mR$-order. For $\mB$ is at the same time an $\mR$-order,
hence an $\mR$-module. If $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_\rho$ form a module basis of $\mA$
with respect to $\mR$, and $\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_\sigma$ one of $\mB$ with respect to
$\mA$, then the products $\alpha_i\beta_k$ plainly form a module basis of $\mB$ with
respect to $\mR$.

3. An element $\alpha$ of $\mT$ is called integral with respect to $\mR$, briefly integral,
if the order $\mR_\alpha$ derived from $\alpha$ is finite — equivalently, if the divisor
chain of modules
\[
        \mA_\mu=(\alpha^0,\alpha,\ldots,\alpha^{\mu-1})
\]
terminates after finitely many steps,
$\mA_n=\mA_{n+1}=\cdots=\mA_{n+\nu}=\cdots$.

The definition also admits a second formulation, to be used in 4.: an element
$\alpha$ of $\mT$ is called integral if there is at least one principal module $\mC$
different from the zero module — i.e. $\mC$ is derived from an element $\gamma\ne0$
— such that the product of the order $\mR_\alpha$ with $\mC$ is a finite module; in
other words, such that the chain of modules $\mC\mA_\mu$ terminates after finitely
many steps.\footnote{If zero divisors are admitted in $\mR$, then the existence of a regular
principal module must be required; that is, $\gamma$ must be assumed to be a
non-zero-divisor, a regular element, which in rings without zero divisors is simply
$\gamma\ne0$.}

Finally the definition is also identical with the usual formulation: an element
$\alpha$ of $\mT$ is called integral if it satisfies at least one equation
\[
        \alpha^n+r_1\alpha^{n-1}+\cdots+r_n=0,
\]
where the $r$'s are elements of $\mR$.

These definitions are in fact identical. Since $\mR_\alpha$ agrees with the module
derived from all powers of $\alpha$, when $\mR_\alpha$ is finite one can always choose
a module basis from these powers. If such a basis is given, for instance, by
$e=\alpha^0,\alpha,\ldots,\alpha^{n-1}$, this means that the chain of the $\mA_\mu$
terminates at $\mA_n$; conversely, $\mA_n=\mA_{n+\nu}\cdots$ gives this basis. But the
termination of the chain of the $\mA_\mu$ is also identical with the existence of the
equation
\[
        \alpha^n+r_1\alpha^{n+1}+\cdots+r_n=0.
\]
If $\mR_\alpha$ is finite, then $\mC\mR_\alpha$ is finite, so the chain $\mC\mA_\mu$
terminates; conversely, from the finiteness of $\mC\mR_\alpha$, hence from the
termination of the $\mC\mA_\mu$, it follows that the $\mA_\mu$ also terminate and
hence that $\mR_\alpha$ is finite. Indeed, since $\mC$ is assumed to be a principal
module distinct from the zero module, $\mC\mA_n=\mC\mA_{n+\nu}$ also gives
$\mA_n=\mA_{n+\nu}$.

The ring property of the integral quantities and the transitive law of integral
dependence rest on the following

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Lemma.} Let $\mS$ be a finite order in $\mT$, and let $\alpha$ be any
element of $\mS$. Then the order $\mR_\alpha$ derived from $\alpha$ is finite; in other
words, all elements of a finite order are integral.

The proof follows by the usual arguments. Let $\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_n$ be a
module basis of $\mS$. Since $\mS$ is a ring, $\alpha\sigma_i$ belongs to $\mS$. Thus
\[
        \alpha\sigma_i=r_{i1}\sigma_1+\cdots+r_{in}\sigma_n,
\]
and hence
\[
        \left|r_{ik}-\alpha e_{ik}\right|=0,
\]
where $e_{ik}=0$ for $i\ne k$ and $e_{ii}=e$; this proves that $\alpha$ is integral.
For the vanishing of the determinant one uses the hypothesis that $\mT$, and hence
$\mS$, has no zero divisors.\footnote{If the divisor-chain condition is assumed in
$\mR$ but zero divisors are admitted, then the lemma is an immediate consequence of
the module theorem in §2, according to which the chain condition transfers to the
modules from $\mS$. This proof too is due, for the number field, to Dedekind
(4th ed., §173, III) and is the first application of chain conditions in the literature.
In the consequences to be given under 4., Dedekind instead uses the more special
fact that the number of residue classes modulo an ideal in an algebraic number
field is finite.}

The system $\mS$ of all integral quantities in $\mT$ forms an order. It contains $\mR$,
for the elements of $\mR$ are integral, since $\mR$ is a finite $\mR$-module with the
identity element as basis. But $\mS$ also has the ring property: for the order
$\mR_{\alpha,\beta}$ derived from two arbitrary integral quantities $\alpha,\beta$ is equal
to the product $\mR_\alpha\cdot\mR_\beta$, and is therefore finite. Hence, by the lemma,
$\alpha\pm\beta$ and $\alpha\beta$ are integral as elements of a finite order.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Transitive law of integral dependence.} If $\mS$ is an order consisting
of integral quantities, then every element of $\mT$ integral with respect to $\mS$ is also
integral with respect to $\mR$. By definition $\alpha$ satisfies an equation
\[
        \alpha^m+\sigma_1\alpha^{m-1}+\cdots+\sigma_m=0,
\]
and is therefore also integral with respect to the order
$\overline{\mS}=\mR_{\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m}$ derived from
$\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m$, which is finite as the product
$\mR_{\sigma_1}\cdot\mR_{\sigma_2}\cdot\ldots\cdot\mR_{\sigma_m}$. By the transitive law
given in 2., the finite $\overline{\mS}$-order $\overline{\mS}_\alpha$ derived from
$\alpha$ is therefore also a finite $\mR$-order, and $\alpha$ is integral by the lemma
as an element of a finite order.

4. The ring $\mR$ is called integrally closed in $\mT$ if every element of $\mT$ integral
with respect to $\mR$ belongs to $\mR$. According to the second formulation of the
definition of integral quantities in 3., an element $\alpha$ of $\mT$ belongs to $\mR$ if
and only if there is at least one principal module $\mC$ different from the zero module
such that $\mC\cdot\mR_\alpha$ is a finite module.\footnote{If zero divisors are admitted in
$\mR$, $\mC$ must again be assumed to be a regular principal module; likewise the
element $c$ in Consequence I must be assumed regular.}

From the concept of an integrally closed ring Dedekind (3rd ed., §172) drew two
important consequences, which make it possible to use this concept for ideal theory:

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Dedekind consequence I.} Let $\mR$ be integrally closed in $\mT$, and
assume further that the divisor-chain condition for ideals (axiom I) holds in $\mR$.
If $\beta$ is a nonintegral element of $\mT$, and $c\ne0$ an element of $\mR$, then
there is an exponent $\sigma\ge0$ such that in the sequence of elements
\[
        c,\;c\beta,\ldots,c\beta^r,\ldots
\]
the elements $c,\ldots,c\beta^\sigma$ are integral, and all the remaining ones are
nonintegral.

If all elements $c\beta^r$ were integral, then by the integral closedness of $\mR$
they would belong to $\mR$. Then the chain of modules
$\mC\mB_1,\mC\mB_2,\ldots,\mC\mB_r,\ldots$ — where $\mC$ denotes the module
derived from $c$, and $\mB_r$ the one derived from
$\beta^0,\ldots,\beta^{r-1}$ — would become a chain of ideals
$\mc_1,\ldots,\mc_r,\ldots$, with
$\mc_r=(c,c\beta,\ldots,c\beta^{r-1})$, which terminates by hypothesis. But then,
contrary to the assumption, $\mR_\beta$ would be finite and $\beta$ integral; hence
some of the elements $c\beta^r$ are nonintegral. Furthermore, with any one
nonintegral element all subsequent ones are nonintegral: for if $c\beta^\tau$ is
integral, hence belongs to $\mR$, then for $\rho<\tau$ the element $c\beta^\rho$ satisfies
\[
        (c\beta^\rho)^\tau-c^{\,\tau-\rho}(c\beta^\tau)^\rho=0
\]
and is therefore integral. Thus if $c\beta^{\sigma+1}$ is the first nonintegral element,
then — since $c$ is integral — $\sigma\ge0$, and this is the required exponent.

Specializing the extension ring $\mT$ to the quotient field of $\mR$ — that is, to the
field obtained by adjoining all pairs of elements\footnote{In Steinitz's familiar
formulation, J. f. M. 137. If zero divisors are admitted in $\mR$, then the quotient
ring must replace the quotient field, by adjoining all quotient pairs whose denominator
is a regular element of $\mR$. Here Dedekind consequence II is no longer valid, for
$n$ will not in general be a regular element.} — gives the following:

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Dedekind consequence II.} Let $\mR$ be integrally closed in its
quotient field $\mK$, and let the divisor-chain condition for ideals hold in $\mR$. If
$\beta$ is a nonintegral element of $\mK$, then $\beta$ admits a representation as
a quotient of elements of $\mR$,
\[
        \beta=m/n,
\]
such that $m^2/n$ is also nonintegral.

Put $\beta=b/c$ with $c\ne0$. Then in the sequence
$c,c\beta=b,c\beta^2,\ldots$ the first two elements are certainly integral, so
$\sigma\ge1$, where $\sigma$ is the exponent of Consequence I. If one sets
$m=c\beta^\sigma$, $n=c\beta^{\sigma-1}$ — because $\sigma\ge1$, these are integral
quantities of the sequence — then $m/n=\beta$, and
$m^2/n=c\beta^{\sigma+1}$ is nonintegral.

The transitive law of integral closedness has the following form: if $\mR$ is any
ring in $\mT$ and $\mS$ denotes the system of all quantities in $\mT$ integral with respect
to $\mR$, then $\mS$ also consists of all quantities of $\mT$ integral with respect to $\mS$.
For every quantity integral with respect to $\mS$ is also integral with respect to $\mR$,
by the transitive law in 3., and hence belongs to $\mS$.
